        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <h2 id="storage-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Storage and Space Planning for a Folding Treadmill
        </h2>
        <p>
          Buying a folding treadmill without measuring your actual space
          first is one of the most common mistakes we see. "Folds up small"
          is relative, and the difference between a 27-inch-wide footprint
          and a 36-inch-wide one matters a lot when you're working with a
          narrow hallway or a tight bedroom corner. Before you buy, measure
          the exact spot where the folded unit will live — width, depth, and
          especially ceiling height, since most folded treadmills stand
          between <strong>5.5 and 6.5 feet</strong> tall on their transport
          wheels. A folded treadmill that's a few inches taller than expected
          can hit a sloped ceiling, a low shelf, or a ceiling fan you forgot
          to account for.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under-bed and closet storage is possible but only with the right
          kind of machine. Most folding treadmills fold the deck upward
          toward the console and are designed to stand vertically, not slide
          flat under furniture. Only a small number of models, generally
          ones marketed as under-desk or walking-pad hybrids, fold flat
          enough to fit under a bed frame with typical clearance. If
          under-bed storage is a hard requirement for you, check that
          specifically before buying rather than assuming any "folding"
          model will work that way.
        </p>
        <p>
          Moving a folded treadmill across your home is its own consideration.
          Nearly every model in this category includes integrated transport
          wheels on the base, but how well those wheels perform depends on
          your flooring. On carpet, especially thicker or plush styles, we
          noticed more resistance and recommend tilting the unit back
          slightly to get the wheels to roll rather than dig in. On hardwood,
          tile, or laminate, the wheels moved smoothly, though we'd still
          recommend a thin protective mat under frequently-traveled paths to
          avoid scuff marks over time. Thresholds and small floor transitions
          were generally not an issue, but very low-clearance wheels on some
          budget models struggled slightly with thick rug edges.
        </p>
        <p>
          Apartment and condo dwellers should also think about noise and
          vibration transfer to units below, both during use and during the
          fold-and-roll process itself. A quality equipment mat placed
          underneath the treadmill's full footprint, folded or unfolded,
          does double duty here: it dampens motor vibration during workouts
          and cushions the impact of wheels rolling across hardwood or tile.
          We noticed a measurable drop in perceived noise on every unit we
          tested once a mat was added, and several testers who share walls
          or floors with neighbors called it a near-essential accessory
          rather than an optional extra. If you're on an upper floor of a
          multi-unit building, budget an extra <strong>$30-$60</strong> for a
          proper equipment mat alongside whichever treadmill you choose.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
