          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Are folding treadmills less durable than non-folding models?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Not inherently. The fold hinge is an added mechanical point,
              but on quality machines it's engineered to handle thousands of
              fold cycles. Durability differences usually come down to
              overall build quality and motor class rather than the presence
              of a folding hinge itself, which is why we test fold points
              specifically for wobble or looseness over repeated use.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Can I fold my treadmill right after finishing a workout?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It's better to let the motor and deck cool for <strong>
                10-15 minutes
              </strong> first. Folding a still-warm unit traps heat around the
              motor housing and belt, which can accelerate wear over time.
              Most manufacturers note this in their manuals, and it's a habit
              worth building even though it's tempting to fold immediately.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do folding treadmills have lower weight capacities than
              standard treadmills?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Often, yes, especially on ultra-compact and budget models,
              where lighter frame materials help keep the folded unit easy
              to lift and move. Mid-range and higher-end folding treadmills
              can still reach <strong>300 pounds or more</strong>, so weight
              capacity should be checked model by model rather than assumed
              based on the folding feature alone.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Is a hydraulic-assist fold worth paying more for?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              If you'll be folding the treadmill daily or multiple times a
              week, yes. Hydraulic or gas-strut assisted folding meaningfully
              reduces the physical effort and the risk of the deck slamming
              down, which matters most for anyone with back issues, smaller
              frame strength, or a household where multiple people will be
              operating it.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Can a folding treadmill be stored under a bed?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Only a small subset of models are designed to fold flat rather
              than fold upright, and those are the only ones suited to
              under-bed storage. Most folding treadmills fold the deck up
              toward the console and are meant to stand vertically on their
              wheels, not slide underneath furniture, so check the fold
              style before assuming under-bed storage is an option.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
